  </aside>
  
  <article class="content">
    <h1>Reactivity System</h1>
    <p class="lead">Pulse uses fine-grained reactivity with signals - the most efficient reactive primitive for surgical DOM updates.</p>

    <section id="signals">
      <h2>createSignal</h2>
      <p>The foundation of Pulse reactivity. A signal holds a value and tracks all places that read it.</p>
      
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-header">
          <span class="label">Basic Usage</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>// Create a signal with initial value
const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);

// Read the value (call the getter)
console.log(count());  // 0

// Update the value
setCount(5);           // Direct value
setCount(c => c + 1);  // Updater function</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="info-box">
        <h4>💡 Why Call the Getter?</h4>
        <p>Unlike React's useState, signals are functions. This enables fine-grained tracking - Pulse knows exactly which DOM nodes depend on which signals.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Signal Types</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code is:raw>// Primitive values
const [name, setName] = createSignal('');
const [age, setAge] = createSignal(0);
const [active, setActive] = createSignal(false);

// Objects (immutable updates recommended)
const [user, setUser] = createSignal({ name: '', email: '' });
setUser(prev => ({ ...prev, name: 'John' }));

// Arrays
const [items, setItems] = createSignal([]);
setItems(prev => [...prev, newItem]);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="effects">
      <h2>createEffect</h2>
      <p>Run side effects automatically when dependencies change. Perfect for logging, API calls, or DOM manipulation.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-header">
          <span class="label">Basic Effect</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code is:raw>const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);

// This runs whenever count() changes
createEffect(() => {
  console.log('Count is now:', count());
});

setCount(1);  // Logs: "Count is now: 1"
setCount(2);  // Logs: "Count is now: 2"</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h3>Effect with Cleanup</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code is:raw>createEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => {
    console.log('Tick');
  }, 1000);
  
  // Return cleanup function
  return () => clearInterval(timer);
});</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <h4>⚠️ Avoid Infinite Loops</h4>
        <p>Don't set a signal inside an effect that reads that same signal, or you'll create an infinite loop.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="memos">
      <h2>createMemo</h2>
      <p>Compute derived values efficiently. Memos cache their result and only recompute when dependencies change.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-header">
          <span class="label">Derived Values</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>const [price, setPrice] = createSignal(100);
const [quantity, setQuantity] = createSignal(2);

// Only recomputes when price or quantity changes
const total = createMemo(() => price() * quantity());

console.log(total());  // 200

setQuantity(5);
console.log(total());  // 500</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h3>Complex Computations</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code is:raw>const [users, setUsers] = createSignal([...]);
const [filter, setFilter] = createSignal('active');

const filteredUsers = createMemo(() => {
  console.log('Filtering...');  // Only runs when deps change
  return users().filter(u => u.status === filter());
});

// Can be used like any signal
filteredUsers().map(u => ...)</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="info-box">
        <h4>🚀 Performance Benefit</h4>
        <p>Memos prevent redundant calculations. If you read a memo 100 times, the computation only runs once per change.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="comparison">
      <h2>When to Use What</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Primitive</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>createSignal</code></td>
            <td>Mutable state</td>
            <td>Form inputs, toggles, counters</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>createMemo</code></td>
            <td>Derived/computed values</td>
            <td>Filtered lists, totals, validation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>createEffect</code></td>
            <td>Side effects</td>
            <td>Logging, API calls, timers</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </article>
</main>

